WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.640
Designing a gene edit once took months of protein engineering, now a short RNA does it in days. This is First Principles Daily, episode 168. Before 2012, hitting a new stretch of deoxyribonucleic acid, DNA, meant building an entirely new protein from scratch. Labs using zinc-finger nucleases, ZFNs, or TALENs, spent weeks or months designing and assembling the binding domains that would recognize each chosen sequence.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.840
experiments. Jennifer Doudna and Immanuel Sharpen-Tears group showed that the Cas9 protein could

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:41.240
instead be directed by a short, synthetic ribonucleic acid, RNA, molecule, whose sequence

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:46.680
alone specified the cut site. The physical requirement had not moved. A double strand

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.560
break still had to land at a chosen address. Only the method of writing that address had

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.488
changed. The older tools worked by fusing a custom DNA binding protein to a nuclease

00:00:57.488 --> 00:01:04.068
domain. Usually Falki. Each new target therefore needed its own protein construct. Someone had

00:01:04.068 --> 00:01:09.988
to redesign the amino acid sequence, express it, purify it, and validate it before a single cut

00:01:09.988 --> 00:01:16.228
could be trusted. A 2024 cell review notes that this protein engineering step limited adoption

00:01:16.228 --> 00:01:25.188
to specialist labs. A 2021 MDPI comparison states that both ZFNs and TALNs demanded laborious design

00:01:25.188 --> 00:01:30.128
in generation of custom proteins for every DNA target. Researchers therefore

00:01:30.128 --> 00:01:34.688
treated each edit as a bespoke project. Cost and duration scaled with the

00:01:34.688 --> 00:01:39.648
difficulty of protein work, not with the cost of ordering DNA. That convention

00:01:39.648 --> 00:01:43.928
held because earlier genome editing successes had all relied on protein DNA

00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:49.288
recognition, so the field kept optimizing inside that frame. Nobody questioned the

00:01:49.288 --> 00:01:53.368
need to build a new protein until the bacterial CRISPR system. Clustered

00:01:53.368 --> 00:01:59.348
regularly interspaced short-palendromic repeats was examined at the level of its molecular mechanism.

00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:04.988
A fair objection sits right there. Any editing tool still needs a nucleus to make the cut.

00:02:05.508 --> 00:02:09.948
The older methods simply bundled recognition and cutting into the same custom molecule.

00:02:10.528 --> 00:02:15.988
That bundling made sense when protein DNA interfaces were the only known way to get specificity.

00:02:15.988 --> 00:02:21.928
So, labs invested in refining zinc-finger arrays, or TAL, your repeats, rather than pulling

00:02:21.928 --> 00:02:22.968
the two jobs apart.

00:02:23.628 --> 00:02:26.128
The rate-limiting step was always the protein itself.

00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:32.048
It's folding, it's expression yield, it's off-target binding, rather than the DNA sequence

00:02:32.048 --> 00:02:32.848
being targeted.

00:02:33.801 --> 00:02:40.601
The 2012 science paper by Jynik Chalinsky, Fanfara, Howard, Doudna, and Sharpentier

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:46.841
Here, established that Cas9 uses a dual RNA structure to locate and cleave DNA at a sequence

00:02:46.841 --> 00:02:47.961
defined by the guide.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:52.061
The physical act required was still a cut at a programmed address.

00:02:52.721 --> 00:02:54.221
Everything else was implementation.

00:02:55.061 --> 00:03:00.581
A 2013 E-Life paper by Jinnick and colleagues then showed that a single guide RNA Chimera

00:03:00.581 --> 00:03:04.821
could program Cas9 to cut human genomic DNA at chosen sites.

00:03:05.261 --> 00:03:09.861
Because the address now wrote on an RNA molecule that could be ordered from a synthesis

00:03:09.861 --> 00:03:15.241
services, the design step collapse from protein engineering to all-ago nucleated synthesis.

00:03:15.941 --> 00:03:21.621
A 2012 Nature Method summary says the system does not require protein engineering for each new target.

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:28.281
It needs only Cas9 plus one hybrid RNA. The 2024 cell review confirms the split.

00:03:28.881 --> 00:03:35.321
CRISPR Cas9 uses guide RNA for targeting, while ZFNs and talons require custom proteins.

00:03:36.001 --> 00:03:38.821
This is where the magic wand number usually earns its keep.

00:03:38.821 --> 00:03:44.021
Wave the wand, arrange the raw atoms into the finished editor for free, and ask

00:03:44.021 --> 00:03:49.421
what is left. The price of the materials alone, the theoretical floor, divide the finished

00:03:49.421 --> 00:03:52.481
price by that floor and you get the first principles ratio.

00:03:53.121 --> 00:03:58.141
Near one, the thing is already close to its physical limit. A large ratio means most of

00:03:58.141 --> 00:04:04.001
the price sits in design, process, organization, or rules. No research source here supplies

00:04:04.001 --> 00:04:09.541
prices for purified Cas9 protein, synthetic RNA, or the earlier custom zinc

00:04:09.541 --> 00:04:14.401
finger or talil arrays. There is nothing to add up and nothing to divide, so a

00:04:14.401 --> 00:04:18.761
numerical magic wand floor and a numerical first principles ratio cannot be

00:04:18.761 --> 00:04:23.541
calculated from the given material. I will not invent one. The gap that can be

00:04:23.541 --> 00:04:27.701
observed is the labor and time previously spent on iterative protein design,

00:04:28.021 --> 00:04:32.681
expression, and testing. Two constraints the RNA shortcut did not remove are

00:04:32.681 --> 00:04:33.641
named in the sources.

00:04:34.261 --> 00:04:38.801
Efficient delivery of the editing machinery into target cells and control of unintended

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:40.541
cuts at similar sequences.

00:04:41.201 --> 00:04:46.401
Another fair objection, Cas9 itself remains a large protein that must still be produced

00:04:46.401 --> 00:04:47.041
or delivered.

00:04:47.521 --> 00:04:50.021
The insight did not eliminate protein synthesis.

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:54.721
It eliminated the need to redesign that protein for every new genomic address.

00:04:55.301 --> 00:05:01.101
Keep the same Cas9 molecule and vary only the short RNA, and labs could test dozens of

00:05:01.101 --> 00:05:03.581
sites in parallel from one protein preparation.

00:05:04.101 --> 00:05:09.401
The experimental variable shifted from which protein was built to which RNA sequence was

00:05:09.401 --> 00:05:09.741
ordered.

00:05:10.281 --> 00:05:14.701
That separation also made it straightforward to ask whether a given cut did the intended

00:05:14.701 --> 00:05:20.701
job or created unwanted edits elsewhere because the same Cas9 preparation could be paired

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:22.981
with many different guides in the same experiment.

00:05:23.935 --> 00:05:29.495
The 2013 demonstration that single-guide RNAs worked in human cells removed the protein

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:35.635
redesign bottleneck for most laboratory experiments. Multiplexing became straightforward. Several

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:41.335
guide RNAs could be supplied together without constructing multiple proteins. A nature communications

00:05:41.335 --> 00:05:47.175
review from 2023 states that delivery methods and off-target activity remained central safety

00:05:47.175 --> 00:05:53.615
considerations for therapeutic use. The cell review from 2024 likewise lists off-target editing

00:05:53.615 --> 00:05:55.715
and delivery constraints as open hurdles.

00:05:56.275 --> 00:06:01.095
For the targeting step itself, the first principles ratio moved closer to the physical

00:06:01.095 --> 00:06:03.515
minimum set by the cost of RNA synthesis.

00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:09.015
For an entire therapeutic edit, the ratio is still dominated by the unsolved steps

00:06:09.015 --> 00:06:11.435
of intracellular delivery and specificity.

00:06:11.935 --> 00:06:15.955
The speed gain in the lab does not automatically become a speed gain in the clinic.

00:06:16.555 --> 00:06:20.275
The sources are clear that the remaining barriers are physical and biological.

00:06:20.275 --> 00:06:25.155
physical, getting the active complex inside the right cells without triggering immune responses

00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:29.815
or lingering too long, rather than further simplifications of the targeting chemistry.

00:06:30.717 --> 00:06:35.737
When recognition is reduced to a molecule whose sequence can be ordered, instead of a protein

00:06:35.737 --> 00:06:40.997
whose fold must be invented, design labor stops being the thing that sets the pace, and

00:06:40.997 --> 00:06:44.497
the problems that were hiding behind that labor become the ones you can see.

00:06:45.057 --> 00:06:46.357
That is the portable piece.

00:06:46.357 --> 00:06:51.217
An inherited method can be excellent at the job it was built for, and still lock the

00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:56.577
next decade inside one frame, because every earlier success used the same kind of interface.

00:06:57.257 --> 00:07:01.117
The people refining zinc finger arrays and teal repeats were not failing.

00:07:01.677 --> 00:07:05.737
They were optimizing the only specificity mechanism the field had evidence for.

00:07:06.317 --> 00:07:11.517
The break came when someone separated addressing from cutting, and asked which half actually

00:07:11.517 --> 00:07:12.737
had to be reinvented.

00:07:12.737 --> 00:07:17.197
Once addressing was a sequence, the same catalytic protein could stay put.

00:07:17.817 --> 00:07:18.577
Practice moved.

00:07:19.197 --> 00:07:23.657
The sources show that the 2012 mechanistic insight was sufficient to change practice

00:07:23.657 --> 00:07:26.377
across thousands of laboratories within a few years.

00:07:26.977 --> 00:07:32.657
The same separation of targeting from catalysis that made CRISPR routine in the lab now defines

00:07:32.657 --> 00:07:35.417
the remaining engineering problems for clinical use.

00:07:36.017 --> 00:07:40.077
Delivery and precision are no longer side notes on a protein design project.

00:07:40.577 --> 00:07:41.657
They are the project.

00:07:41.657 --> 00:07:49.437
which current delivery or specificity barrier will next be restated as a simple physical requirement rather than an inherited constraint.

